<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Usrushana</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ushrusana, Osrushana</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Usrushana was a historical region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khujand">Khujand</a>, south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syr-darya">Syr Darya</a>, in modern northern Tajikistan. Ruled by local princes with the title afshin, it was conquered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> in the early 9th century. Its best-known son was the general al-Afshin, who served the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mutasim">al-Mu'tasim</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sajid-dynasty">Sajid dynasty</a> also came from its military families.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/usrushana.jpg" alt="Usrushana" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bertramz · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bunjikat,Kahkaha1-1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
